Z||000007||gtjeff||08-13-2001||12:39 AM||||Speedster is built by Lotus in I believe Norfolk, England. Plant has capacity for only approx. 10,000 cars. Vauxhall(another GM unit-check their web site)is selling this car as the VX220. There isnt much capacity to import and GM on their media website stated this generation car wouldnt be imported here. <BR>Car seems too small inside and there are quality issues, plant running at 50% speed.<BR>Truth is we dont need it. All GM has to do is use the EV1 Aluminum Chasis to build a new fiero, the tooling for ev1 is just sitting there, last car made in 1999 at Craft Centre. I e-mailed the ev1 website asking if car was based on fiero-they CLAIM its not. But i have seen pictures of the structure on that web site and also listed on a chart elsewhere as being the same as fiero-except for the aluminum composition. This structure would eliminate approx 250 pounds off of fiero's weight. Fiero would then weigh less than Mosler's mid-engine mt900, car has ls1 and 0-60 is 3.6. Chevy division wouldnt like this idea(Zo6 is 4.0 0-60). Why not e-mail Pontiac, tell them to build an aluminum chasis ls1 fiero from the ev1. It is amazing that corporate politics prevent them from selling what would be a world class sports car, after all they are the performance division.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierobear||08-13-2001||02:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtjeff:<BR><B>But i have seen pictures of the structure on that web site and also listed on a chart elsewhere as being the same as fiero-except for the aluminum composition. This structure</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is there any way you can post pix of the bare chassis? I'd be curious to see what it looks like.<P>I talked to Fiero (FOCOA) Phil about the Opel, and he said that GM isn't going to bring it over here because of the crash tests and other DOT certification problems.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Nashco||08-13-2001||03:13 PM||||Actually, several myths here should be put to bed.  The Opel Speedster isn't based on the Lotus Elise...it has several styling and drivetrain cues taken from a Lotus, but GM authorities I've contacted say that it is strictly Opel/GM engineering.  <P>Also, according to a GM safety representative, the Speedster cannot be released in America due to headlight placement that would require an entire rebody, and sub-par front crash standards.<P>I was interested as hell in this car when I first heard about it, and had the opportunity to talk with a high-up in GM safety because he did a speech at our school about something else (My school is formerly known as General Motors Institute, so we get all kinds of neat speakers <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)...it seems we'll never get our hands on any cool cars here!!!  Well..I guess I can always dream and try to make something myself that could compete with that and look half as good.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||Slammed Fiero||08-13-2001||03:56 PM||||My Dad went to GMI for a while.<P>In anycase... Speedvision was where I learned the car is based on the Lotus , as Well From Rob Mims Director of Marketing for lotus Cars USA.<P><BR>There have even been blurbs about it on Autonews.com talking about the relationship between the 2 cars , and in Car and driver. I also heard a lot about it when I worked for GM doing marketing research.<P>I think your guest speaker / source is mistaken.<P>JM <p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
